---
import { CHASSIS, starterLoadout } from "@void-drift/mode-a";
import InstrumentEmbed from "../../../components/lab/instrument/InstrumentEmbed.astro";
import BladeAssembly from "../../../components/studies/fiddler/BladeAssembly.svelte";
import BladeConstruction from "../../../components/studies/fiddler/BladeConstruction.svelte";
import CapacityStudy from "../../../components/studies/fiddler/CapacityStudy.svelte";
import CorvetteVolume from "../../../components/studies/fiddler/CorvetteVolume.svelte";
import EngineStudy from "../../../components/studies/fiddler/EngineStudy.svelte";
import Exploration from "../../../components/studies/fiddler/Exploration.svelte";
import {
	ENGINE_PAIR_MASS,
	ENGINE_PAIR_THRUST,
	FOUR_ENGINE_LOADED_MASS,
	FOUR_ENGINE_MASS,
	FOUR_ENGINE_THRUST,
	TRIAL_EQUIPMENT_MASS,
	TRIAL_HULL_MASS,
	TRIAL_LOADED_MASS,
} from "../../../components/studies/fiddler/engine-placement";
import FlightReady from "../../../components/studies/fiddler/FlightReady.svelte";
import FourEngineStudy from "../../../components/studies/fiddler/FourEngineStudy.svelte";
import LhiForm from "../../../components/studies/fiddler/LhiForm.svelte";
import NoseSideConstruction from "../../../components/studies/fiddler/NoseSideConstruction.svelte";
import PincerSlotStudy from "../../../components/studies/fiddler/PincerSlotStudy.svelte";
import RootPair from "../../../components/studies/fiddler/RootPair.svelte";
import StabilizerStudy from "../../../components/studies/fiddler/StabilizerStudy.svelte";
import WindowStudy from "../../../components/studies/fiddler/WindowStudy.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "lhi-fiddler-2026-11-03");
const fiddlerFlightComparison = {
	version: 1,
	burn: "turn",
	vehicles: [
		{ label: "Fiddler · stabilizers only", loadout: starterLoadout("fiddler") },
		{
			label: "Fiddler · linked engines on",
			loadout: { ...starterLoadout("fiddler"), linkedEngines: true },
		},
	],
};
const waspControlReference = {
	version: 1,
	burn: "turn",
	vehicles: [
		{
			label: "Current Wasp / light ion, empty equipment slots",
			loadout: {
				version: 2,
				chassis: "wasp",
				side: "ion",
				stabilizers: true,
				hull: [null, null],
				aft: [],
			},
		},
	],
};
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section aria-labelledby="problem">
		<h2 id="problem">01 / The heavy end of the LHI family</h2>
		<p>
			<strong
				>Fiddler is a heavy assault corvette that advances behind two long,
				load-bearing blades.</strong
			>
			Where Wasp feels like a slim engine with a pilot, Fiddler should feel like
			a substantial platform with room to prepare for a long, difficult run. Its
			character comes from the open catamaran stance and the weight gathered
			behind it.
		</p>
		<p>
			In the Hangar, fitting two pieces of equipment to each forward strut
			should feel natural: these are useful hulls, not decorative antennae. In
			flight, the gap between them must survive the small scale. Wasp’s cool,
			flat planes, long lit ridges, swept shoulders and compact black canopy
			provide the family resemblance. Four built-in stabilizers belong to the
			brief; their authority on a much heavier hull still needs investigation.
		</p>
		<p>
			The current Corvette has {CHASSIS.corvette.mass} hull mass; Fiddler’s
			current working hull mass is <strong>{TRIAL_HULL_MASS}</strong>, before
			fittings, refined from the initial 180–210 exploration. LHI ships should
			be quite fast: strong forward drive and decisive turning, with a tradeoff
			when combining them. Fiddler commits to a line, turns to choose the next
			one, then pushes again. This round investigates the silhouette, bridge
			position and space for fittings. Thrust, turning, reserves and combat
			function remain open; “assault” describes the intended character, not an
			implemented firing system.
		</p>
		<figure class="reference">
			<a
				href="/studies/lhi-fiddler-2026-11-03/feeling-draft.png"
				aria-label="Open the original Fiddler feeling draft at full size"
			>
				<img
					src="/studies/lhi-fiddler-2026-11-03/feeling-draft.png"
					width="715"
					height="686"
					alt="Original feeling draft: a cool blue twin-blade catamaran on the left, beside a smaller pointed ship on the right; both have swept shoulders and long faceted bodies."
				>
			</a>
			<figcaption>
				<strong>Initial feeling draft · designer reference</strong><br>The two
				forward blades, open middle and shared family language are the starting
				point. Size and exact proportions remain open; the pincer construction
				and cockpit/window framing are family references. For 02.2, the glass
				stays black. Original image preserved unchanged.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>
	</section>
	<h2 id="exploration">02 / LHI form, scale and propulsion</h2>
	<details class="earlier-round">
		<summary>02.1 / First layouts and family lesson — retained</summary>
		<section aria-labelledby="exploration-02-1">
			<h3 id="exploration-02-1">02.1 / Where does the bridge belong?</h3>
			<p>
				<strong>Rejected exploration, retained as evidence.</strong>
				The three proposals and their original reasoning remain below. The
				family-language lesson following them supersedes the recommendation of
				B; no variant was accepted.
			</p>
			<p>
				Three constructions keep the twin forward hulls and four stabilizers. A
				central keel, an offset bridge and a broad stern yoke test where the
				ship’s identity and weight should sit. Each offers the same tentative
				ten-mount arrangement, including two equipment mounts on each forward
				blade, so slot count does not decide the silhouette for us.
			</p>
			<Exploration client:load />
			<p>
				<strong>First direction to inspect: B, the port bridge.</strong>
				It gives the open throat the most space and makes the offset cockpit a
				distinctive feature without making one blade smaller. A is the closer
				Wasp echo; C tests how much stern mass Fiddler can carry before the
				family resemblance weakens. These are proposals, not a settled choice.
			</p>
			<p>
				The mounted and fitted views test whether the bare shapes leave useful,
				quiet surfaces. They cannot establish handling, balance, structural
				strength or the final loadout rules. Once the stance is chosen, the next
				exploration can resolve its bridge, fittings and stabilizer placement
				together.
			</p>
		</section>
		<section aria-labelledby="family-lesson">
			<h3 id="family-lesson">02.1 / Lesson: family comes before layout</h3>
			<p>
				<strong>Ship family and ship role are separate.</strong>
				There are four families: LHI, Daehan, Aegis, and a military-inspired
				family whose name is still open. Wasp and Fiddler belong to LHI; Daehan
				Scout No.7, Aegis XF-1 Interceptor and the military-inspired Mark II
				Escort Frigate establish different lineages. Fiddler being a corvette
				does not put it in the same family as the military-inspired Corvette.
				The latter is a mass and scale comparison here, not an LHI design
				reference.
			</p>
			<p>
				The repository names individual ships but underdevelops the families
				behind them. Shared colours and low-poly rendering do not adequately
				define LHI. The initial draft illustrates a particular family character,
				including the nature of the front pincers and the cockpit/window
				language. Those relationships matter, even while its size and exact
				proportions remain open.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>02.1 reduced that reference too far.</strong>
				Two forward bars, a gap and a black canopy preserved a checklist while
				moving far from the intended forms. Changing the cockpit position did
				not resolve that loss. The earlier caption’s dismissal of canopy
				treatment as a target was too broad: the precise drawing is not binding,
				but its cockpit/window language is part of the reference to understand.
			</p>
			<p>
				The next useful design work is to articulate LHI’s construction, pincer
				and window language from the supplied draft and Wasp before another
				Fiddler layout. The rejected drawings remain visible at the designer’s
				request because they make this gap inspectable. This records the finding
				within this study; it does not establish finished family rules or accept
				any of the proposed hulls or the ten-mount arrangement.
			</p>
		</section>
	</details>
	<details class="earlier-round">
		<summary>
			02.2 / Copied forms and the missing corvette scale — retained
		</summary>
		<section aria-labelledby="exploration-02-2">
			<h3 id="exploration-02-2">02.2 / Recovering LHI’s construction</h3>
			<p>
				<strong>Keep the window black; develop its shape and framing.</strong>
				This settles the glass question for this round. The investigation
				returns to the supplied draft’s bent, widening pincers, diagonal
				shoulder roots and long canopy embedded between hull cheeks. Those are a
				reading of this reference to test, not yet a complete set of rules for
				every LHI ship.
			</p>
			<p>
				The left drawing reconstructs the draft’s forms with flat fills and
				black glass. The right makes one proposal: increase the blades’
				cross-section and the roots’ breadth while keeping the open middle,
				swept shoulders and tapering centre body. Both use the same drawing
				scale. The cockpit stays central so its placement does not distract from
				testing the construction.
			</p>
			<LhiForm client:load />
			<p>
				Only the four requested forward equipment footprints are shown. The
				ten-mount arrangement from 02.1 is not carried forward; the rest of the
				loadout, main engines and stabilizer authority remain unresolved. This
				comparison can establish whether the form belongs to LHI and has room to
				grow into a heavy ship. It cannot establish mass, flight feel or fitting
				clearance for a complete loadout.
			</p>
		</section>
		<section aria-labelledby="corvette-scale">
			<h3 id="corvette-scale">
				02.2 / Clarification: repeated forms, missing ship scale
			</h3>
			<p>
				The original draft deliberately copies Wasp’s cockpit one-to-one and
				reuses Wasp’s front polygons for the pincers. Repeating those forms on a
				larger ship is the family-building intent. The unresolved design problem
				is that the result does not yet feel like a corvette: a substantial, big
				ship. Recovering the copied forms in 02.2 does not solve that problem.
			</p>
			<p>
				A working diagnosis is that much of the extra footprint reads as long
				extensions, thin swept surfaces and space between them, while the
				central body remains slender. The cockpit already provides a familiar
				scale reference; enlarging or shrinking it is not the starting
				correction. The next investigation should keep the repeated LHI forms
				legible while giving the equipment-bearing hulls and their connections
				convincing volume. The catamaran opening remains part of the brief.
				Where that volume belongs, and how much it needs, are still design
				questions.
			</p>
		</section>
	</details>
	<details class="earlier-round">
		<summary>
			02.3 / Volume comparisons and the surface-language lesson — retained
		</summary>
		<section aria-labelledby="exploration-02-3">
			<h3 id="exploration-02-3">
				02.3 / Where does the corvette carry its volume?
			</h3>
			<h4 id="surface-language-lesson">
				Retained lesson / Surface language before volume comparison
			</h4>
			<p>
				The volume question is useful, but these drawings do not yet make it
				answerable. Their planes do not clearly communicate which surfaces
				belong to a raised body, a thin wing, a bevel or a continuous deck.
				Copying bow polygons and cockpit outlines alone does not preserve that
				understanding. These 02.3 examples are retained at the designer’s
				request as evidence of this gap; neither volume direction is accepted.
			</p>
			<p>
				Whole-ship variants were paused to establish the shape language. The
				isolated construction in 02.4 now takes that discussion forward. First
				establish the parts, their surface relationships and how light describes
				them; then compare where Fiddler gains volume. The existing ship drawing
				fundamentals already call for solid objects expressed through a few flat
				planes, with light from the ship’s left and above. This round needs to
				apply that foundation to LHI’s specific forms.
			</p>
			<p>
				The
				<a href="/lab/studies/lhi-wasp#ab306-refinements">accepted Wasp 06.9</a>
				provides a concrete reference: a slender faceted body, a black canopy
				between its cheeks, thin swept wing surfaces and a tapering, flat-ended
				tail wedge. The side and rear winglets share a shade and a physical
				bridge, with parallel trailing edges, so they read as one continuing
				plane. These are relationships to understand before adapting them to the
				larger hull; their exact Fiddler proportions remain open.
			</p>
			<h4 id="blade-section">
				Agreed starting form / Wasp’s nose continued into each blade
			</h4>
			<p>
				Each forward blade begins with the same implied three-dimensional form
				as Wasp’s nose: its ridge and sloping sides continue along the blade.
				The shared nose section, including the angles of its faces, is the
				starting reference. A continuous flat upper deck is not required. This
				carries the construction behind the copied front polygons through the
				length of the part.
			</p>
			<p>
				The initial draft also includes a triangular upper surface near the rear
				end of each blade. Its transition into the blade and root remains to be
				resolved locally. Equipment placement must follow the blade’s
				established section; its exact mounting arrangement remains open.
			</p>
			<p>
				With the same face angles and the same ship-relative light,
				corresponding surfaces should retain Wasp’s light-and-dark relationship
				along the blade. A new shade needs a change of surface angle or a
				separate object to explain it. This settles the starting blade
				construction, while the larger hull’s proportions and the source of its
				corvette scale remain unresolved.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>The retained experiment.</strong>
				The first 02.3 examples did not provide a useful comparison because
				their connection to LHI was lost. These replacements carry over the
				actual front bow faces and the complete command body from the draft
				reconstruction. The canopy, its framing, the long body taper and aft
				vanes keep their original shape and size. The two bow faces move apart
				without changing shape; the new volume starts behind them.
			</p>
			<CorvetteVolume client:load />
			<p>
				This comparison attempted to investigate
				<strong>where the major hull volume belongs</strong>. A deepens the
				angular pincers and their swept roots. B puts a broader, deeper shoulder
				deck beneath the original command body. The Lineage view isolates the
				unchanged parts so family continuity can be checked directly. The same
				fitting count provides a size reference and exposes crowding, but it
				cannot resolve the unclear surface construction.
			</p>
			<p>
				The 180–210 hull-mass range remains provisional. These drawings do not
				calculate mass or establish handling, structural strength or a final
				loadout. Engine positions and stabilizer authority need a later flight
				investigation after the hull direction is useful.
			</p>
		</section>
	</details>
	<details class="earlier-round">
		<summary>02.4 / Ridge and lighting explanation — retained</summary>
		<section aria-labelledby="exploration-02-4">
			<h3 id="exploration-02-4">02.4 / Wasp’s nose becomes a blade</h3>
			<p>
				The agreed starting point is a ridge with the same sloping sides as
				Wasp’s nose, continued along each blade. This isolated construction uses
				Wasp’s actual nose outline and face colours. Switch between Top and
				Angled to inspect how the two flat faces describe one solid part.
			</p>
			<BladeConstruction client:load />
			<p>
				This tests one direct continuation: the nose’s taper and face angles
				continue toward a broader root. The original artwork defines a top view,
				so the height shown here is an illustrative interpretation shared by
				both pieces, not an established Wasp measurement. The blade length is a
				study fixture. The triangular surface near the root, fittings and
				whole-ship proportions remain unresolved; this round first makes the
				ridge and its lighting inspectable.
			</p>
			<p>
				The construction explanation was received positively. The reference was
				then clarified: “nose” includes the nose face and the longer side beside
				the cockpit, with a change of angle between them. 02.5 carries that full
				sequence forward. The nose-only extension above remains the earlier,
				incomplete reading of the intended body.
			</p>
		</section>
	</details>
	<details class="earlier-round">
		<summary>02.5 / The original nose and side — retained</summary>
		<section aria-labelledby="exploration-02-5">
			<h3 id="exploration-02-5">02.5 / Nose, bend, long side</h3>
			<p>
				LHI’s shared language includes the nose, its turn into the longer side,
				and the complex inner contour beside the cockpit. The reference here is
				<strong>one side of Wasp</strong>: the two actual polygons, with the
				cockpit opening and the fuselage behind that side cut away. Both inner
				and outer edges belong to the form being carried into Fiddler.
			</p>
			<NoseSideConstruction client:load />
			<p>
				The earlier solid study filled that opening and mirrored the outside
				into a complete body, losing the inner shape. This corrected extraction
				keeps the original two faces intact. Wasp gives them the same shade;
				their edges carry the change of direction. This is the family
				resemblance to preserve as proportions and arrangements change. The
				angled view’s height remains illustrative; the inner wall, triangular
				root, fittings and complete Fiddler hull remain to be designed.
			</p>
		</section>
	</details>
	<details class="earlier-round">
		<summary>02.6 / Wall, root and mirrored pair — retained</summary>
		<section aria-labelledby="exploration-02-6">
			<h3 id="exploration-02-6">02.6 / From copied side to forward blade</h3>
			<p>
				<strong
					>The wall loses the recognizable form; the root does not.</strong
				>
				The mirrored pair is worth exploring further. This separates three
				different outcomes: the added wall is rejected, the root preserves the
				family resemblance, and the paired arrangement remains a promising
				direction. The original comparison remains below; 02.7 isolates the
				useful parts without the wall.
			</p>
			<p>
				The two Wasp faces now form the exposed side of a blade. A return wall
				follows their complex inner contour, and a triangular upper surface
				joins the rear of the side to a broader root. Switch through Faces, Wall
				and Root to see each addition; the original two polygons stay unchanged.
			</p>
			<BladeAssembly client:load />
			<p>
				This proposal tests whether the copied LHI side can become a
				substantial, connected part while its nose, bend and inner contour stay
				recognizable. The root grows locally behind the long side. The paired
				view establishes the open stance and an aft connection to develop
				further; it does not yet establish corvette scale or a complete loadout.
				Mass and flight behaviour remain outside this construction round.
			</p>
		</section>
	</details>
	<details class="earlier-round">
		<summary>02.7 / Mirrored faces and roots — retained</summary>
		<section aria-labelledby="exploration-02-7">
			<h3 id="exploration-02-7">
				02.7 / The pair, with the copied edge exposed
			</h3>
			<p>
				The two mirrored Wasp sides establish the open stance. Adding only the
				triangular roots gives each a broader rear attachment while keeping the
				original nose, bend and long side visible. Both examples use the same
				size, spacing and lighting; the root is the only change.
			</p>
			<RootPair client:load />
			<p>
				The wall from 02.6 is absent from both examples. This comparison
				isolates the useful paired form before developing the hull behind it.
				Root dimensions and spacing remain provisional; corvette scale, cockpit
				position and equipment space are still open.
			</p>
		</section>
	</details>
	<details class="earlier-round">
		<summary>02.8 / The connected LHI sketch — retained</summary>
		<section aria-labelledby="exploration-02-8">
			<h3 id="exploration-02-8">
				02.8 / The blades become part of an LHI ship
			</h3>
			<p>
				This new sketch brings the copied forms into a complete composition. The
				mirrored blades, swept roots and long cockpit body share a sequence of
				tapered face, bend and longer side. The family resemblance now carries
				through their connections as well as the individual parts. This is a
				promising direction; the complete Fiddler design remains open.
			</p>
			<figure class="connected-reference">
				<a
					href="/studies/lhi-fiddler-2026-11-03/connected-draft.png"
					aria-label="Open the new connected Fiddler sketch at full size"
				>
					<img
						src="/studies/lhi-fiddler-2026-11-03/connected-draft.png"
						width="398"
						height="783"
						alt="New Fiddler sketch: two long forward blades connect through swept, angular shoulders to a central cockpit body with a long tapered tail and two rear fins."
					>
				</a>
				<figcaption>
					<p><strong>New feeling draft · original preserved</strong></p>
					<p>
						<strong>Repeated faces.</strong>
						The blades retain the nose-to-side bend and the complex inner
						contour. The cockpit framing repeats that angular language in the
						centre.
					</p>
					<p>
						<strong>Swept connections.</strong>
						Each blade widens into a pointed root, with diagonal surfaces
						reaching the central body. Those connections echo Wasp’s shoulders
						and give the whole silhouette a coherent sweep.
					</p>
					<p>
						<strong>Open stance.</strong>
						The long gap between the blades stays clear, while the rear fins and
						tapered centre give the composition a second, smaller repetition of
						the same angles.
					</p>
					<p>
						The coloured glass belongs to the supplied reference. Black glass
						remains the working decision for the ship.
					</p>
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<details class="earlier-round">
				<summary>Family line-up · supplied comparison with Wasp</summary>
				<figure class="lineup-reference">
					<a href="/studies/lhi-fiddler-2026-11-03/family-lineup.png">
						<img
							src="/studies/lhi-fiddler-2026-11-03/family-lineup.png"
							width="1626"
							height="1022"
							alt="Supplied line-up of two Fiddler sketches around Wasp, with similar cockpit sizes and shared angular faces."
						>
					</a>
					<figcaption>
						The copied cockpit gives the three drawings a common size reference.
						Original comparison preserved unchanged.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
			</details>
			<p>
				<strong
					>The family reads more clearly; corvette scale still needs
					evidence.</strong
				>
				The prominent cockpit and long, narrow tail give this sketch an agile
				character. The next useful comparison would preserve these angles and
				use Wasp’s cockpit size and equipment footprints as scale references,
				including two fittings on each blade. That would reveal how much hull is
				available around familiar-sized parts before changing the silhouette.
				The sketch alone does not settle the 180–210 mass target or the four
				stabilizers’ placement.
			</p>
		</section>
	</details>
	<details class="earlier-round">
		<summary>02.9 / Equipment and hull scale — retained</summary>
		<section aria-labelledby="exploration-02-9">
			<h3 id="exploration-02-9">02.9 / Put the equipment on it</h3>
			<p>
				The family language is sufficient to investigate capacity. Fiddler must
				carry more than the existing cruiser while retaining the copied blade
				bends, swept roots and cockpit framing. The same equipment is drawn at
				the same scale on both hulls, so useful space can be judged directly.
			</p>
			<CapacityStudy client:load />
			<p>
				The first trial adds two non-engine positions over the cruiser: eight
				places for equipment, plus two engines. Two of those equipment positions
				sit on each blade. This is a capacity hypothesis, not an accepted
				loadout. The Fiddler hull is a procedural trace of the new sketch, with
				black glass.
			</p>
			<p>
				At Draft size, the round coils overhang the blades and crowd the
				shoulder connections; the rear engines also sit close to the central
				equipment. Hull +35% gives the items more separation, while the coil
				overhang keeps the width of the mounting surfaces an open question. This
				is a size comparison, not a mass calculation: the 180–210 target, four
				stabilizers, final cockpit size and engine authority remain separate
				design questions.
			</p>
		</section>
	</details>
	<details class="earlier-round">
		<summary>
			02.10 / Window size and the accepted hull scale — retained
		</summary>
		<section aria-labelledby="exploration-02-10">
			<h3 id="exploration-02-10">02.10 / Move and size the LHI window</h3>
			<p>
				LHI keeps Wasp’s black window shape. Start at its original size, then
				move and scale it directly on the larger Fiddler hull. A familiar window
				on a larger body lets us explore both the ship’s scale and the bridge’s
				place within it.
			</p>
			<WindowStudy client:load />
			<p>
				<strong>Designer decision:</strong>
				carry forward the +35% hull with the exact Wasp-shaped black window at
				100% Wasp size (19.5 × 6 units). That familiar-sized opening resolves
				the perceived scale of the ship. The tool remains available; an edited
				setting is not a new decision. The following round uses the central
				starting position. Exact window placement, equipment mounts and flight
				values remain provisional.
			</p>
		</section>
	</details>
	<details class="earlier-round">
		<summary>
			02.11 / Two-engine placement and the LHI handling principle — retained
		</summary>
		<section aria-labelledby="exploration-02-11">
			<h3 id="exploration-02-11">02.11 / Push, turn, then push again</h3>
			<p>
				This two-engine comparison led to the four-engine arrangement in 02.12:
				the designer chose to combine the blade-root and rear pairs.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong
					>LHI’s handling identity: quite fast, with strong forward drive and
					strong turning, with little ability to combine them.</strong
				>
				Fiddler should point its nose decisively, then commit to the new
				direction. Turning the hull does not immediately turn its existing
				travel; the next main burn redirects it.
			</p>
			<p>
				Wasp already separates these jobs: both controls fire the main engine;
				one control turns on the stabilizers. The proposal here extends that to
				two parallel main engines, firing together only on both controls. Their
				separation is a packaging choice, not differential steering. Stabilizers
				can still brake residual spin while the main engines fire, as on Wasp.
			</p>
			<EngineStudy client:load />
			<h4>Size the drive for the fitted ship</h4>
			<p>
				The working hull mass is now exactly {TRIAL_HULL_MASS}. These two
				engines add {ENGINE_PAIR_MASS} and the eight trial fittings add
				{TRIAL_EQUIPMENT_MASS}:
				<strong>{TRIAL_LOADED_MASS} total</strong>. With today’s heavy-ion
				values, the pair supplies {ENGINE_PAIR_THRUST.toLocaleString("en-US")}
				force units. Dividing by that mass gives about
				{Math.round(ENGINE_PAIR_THRUST / TRIAL_LOADED_MASS)}
				u/s² of initial forward acceleration at full power, from rest. This is a
				force budget; actual acceleration tapers with speed, and power
				availability matters. Lower mass helps acceleration; the engine speed
				ceiling is a separate limit. The fast LHI character still needs to be
				judged in flight.
			</p>
			<p>
				Two heavy ions are a starting hypothesis. Moving them cannot solve a
				thrust shortage. Strong turning will separately depend on stabilizer
				force, leverage and this larger hull’s rotational inertia; four slits
				alone do not establish it. Main-engine placement comes first, then the
				four stabilizers can be arranged around those engines and their exhaust.
			</p>
			<details>
				<summary>
					Inspect Wasp’s current push–turn–push control reference
				</summary>
				<p>
					B2 runs six seconds of both controls, two of left only, one of coast,
					then five of both. This is the current Wasp, not simulated Fiddler.
					The shared instrument cannot yet represent Fiddler’s hull mass, paired
					main drive and four-stabilizer geometry; that extension is proposed
					before making Fiddler flight comparisons.
				</p>
				<InstrumentEmbed
					config={waspControlReference}
					label="Current Wasp control reference"
				/>
			</details>
		</section>
	</details>
	<details class="earlier-round">
		<summary>02.12 / Four engines, one forward drive — accepted</summary>
		<section aria-labelledby="exploration-02-12">
			<h3 id="exploration-02-12">02.12 / Four engines, one forward drive</h3>
			<p>
				<strong>Accepted arrangement:</strong>
				the four main-engine positions, underwing blade-root mounting and two
				linked engine-only pairs fit the ship’s character. Engine type remains
				selectable within each pair.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Designer direction: use both pairs.</strong>
				Two engine slots sit below the blade-root wings at spacing 47 and
				fore–aft +1; two more sit at the rear at spacing 16 and fore–aft +39.
				Spacing is centre-to-centre, so the mount centres are (−23.5, +1),
				(+23.5, +1), (−8, +39) and (+8, +39). All four exhaust straight aft.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>All four slots accept engines only.</strong>
				E1 and E2 form one linked pair; E3 and E4 form the other. Both slots in
				a pair must carry the same engine type. The two pairs may use different
				types, while every fit remains left–right symmetrical.
			</p>
			<FourEngineStudy client:load />
			<p>
				This keeps the +35% hull, Wasp-sized black window and {TRIAL_HULL_MASS}
				hull mass. The eight trial equipment positions remain available for
				review, giving twelve fitting positions with the four engines. The
				underwing roots preserve the visible wing surfaces; their exhaust paths
				and the four stabilizers still need to be checked together.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Reference budget: four heavy ions.</strong>
				Engine choices remain open within the linked pairs. With four current
				heavy ions as the trial fit, engine mass is {FOUR_ENGINE_MASS}; adding
				the eight trial fittings’
				{TRIAL_EQUIPMENT_MASS}
				gives <strong>{FOUR_ENGINE_LOADED_MASS} total mass</strong>. Their
				combined {FOUR_ENGINE_THRUST.toLocaleString("en-US")} force units give
				about {Math.round(FOUR_ENGINE_THRUST / FOUR_ENGINE_LOADED_MASS)} u/s² of
				initial acceleration from rest at full power. This is a thrust budget,
				not a flight result. More ion engines increase acceleration strongly;
				the current ion speed-ceiling bonus has diminishing returns.
			</p>
			<p>
				All four mains are intended to fire together on both controls. One
				control turns through the separate stabilizers, preserving LHI’s
				push–turn–push character. Stabilizer authority and sustained power
				supply remain the next handling questions.
			</p>
		</section>
	</details>
	<section aria-labelledby="exploration-02-13">
		<h3 id="exploration-02-13">02.13 / Place the four turning thrusters</h3>
		<p>
			LHI’s four turning thrusters are built into the ship and always enabled,
			as on Wasp. They fire when turning or braking rotation; they are not
			optional fittings or continuously burning engines. The accepted hull,
			window and four main-engine positions stay fixed while these two mirrored
			pairs are placed.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Decided placement:</strong>
			front pair spacing 48, fore–aft −43, slit tilt 8.5°; rear pair spacing 29,
			fore–aft +33, slit tilt −16.5°. Spacing is centre-to-centre. These
			corrected values replace the miscopied 27 front spacing and +19.5° rear
			tilt, and are carried into 02.14.
		</p>
		<StabilizerStudy client:load />
		<p>
			The placement tool now opens at the decision. Further edits here are
			experiments; Reset to decision restores the settled values. Turning
			strength remains an open flight question for the shared instrument
			extension.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="exploration-02-14">
		<h3 id="exploration-02-14">02.14 / Fit the two pincer slot pairs</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Decided pincer slots:</strong>
			both pairs have spacing 31, with centres at x = ±15.5. The forward pair
			sits at fore–aft −52 and uses
			<strong>+ · weapon or support</strong>; the aft pair sits at fore–aft −29
			and uses <strong>S · support only</strong>. All four are mounted below the
			ship. Neither pair accepts engines; S slots also exclude weapons.
			Positions are mirrored, while fittings remain independent.
		</p>
		<PincerSlotStudy client:load />
		<p>
			The editor now starts at the decided positions and under-ship mounting.
			Reset to decided slots restores those choices while keeping trial
			fittings. Weapons and support use their actual game artwork and size;
			weapons are static previews. Fitted view shows the hull covering the parts
			mounted below. The final centre slot is added in 03 below. Operating
			clearance remains to be reviewed in flight.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="readiness">
		<h2 id="readiness">03 / Ready for an integrated flight trial</h2>
		<FlightReady client:visible />
		<p>
			The designer accepts the ship’s form and requests flight integration after
			one final centre slot. That slot accepts a weapon or support, sits below
			the hull at x 0 / fore–aft +20, and occupies the space between the
			Wasp-sized window and rear engines. +20 is the implementation starting
			point for this last slot. The resulting ship has nine slots: two linked
			engine pairs, two independent front + slots, two independent S slots, and
			the centre + slot.
		</p>
		<p>
			The shared Hangar, renderer, energy system, simulation and flight controls
			can carry the design. Required adaptations are a fifth chassis, five hull
			fittings, a second required mirrored engine pair, support-only
			compatibility, and Wasp-style four-stabilizer control at Fiddler’s
			accepted positions. The hull stays at 187 mass. Both controls fire all
			four mains; one control turns with the stabilizers. With Linked engines
			on, turning or automatic spin braking also fires the two mains on the
			corresponding side, adding forward speed and exhaust without additional
			turning torque. Linking defaults off. There is no unresolved design
			blocker to a trial.
		</p>
		<p>
			The main-menu starting fit is four Light ions, two Potential Wells in the
			lower pincer S slots, and one Gravity coil in the centre + slot below the
			chassis. Both forward + slots start empty. This is 273 assembled mass and
			270 energy capacity.
		</p>
		<InstrumentEmbed
			config={fiddlerFlightComparison}
			label="Fiddler: stabilizers alone or linked drive"
		/>
		<p>
			The shared instrument compares the same default ship with linking off and
			on. It supplies unlimited energy and omits planetary gravity; use the
			Hangar flight for real energy and collision behavior.
		</p>
		<p>
			Initial stabilizer force and energy use follow Wasp; the larger hull’s
			inertia is calculated from its actual bounds. Handling, endurance and
			clearance still need human flight review. Weapon fittings remain static,
			as in the current game. Early Fiddler runs are unranked.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="scope">
		<h2 id="scope">04 / Integrate the accepted ship</h2>
		<p>
			The designer explicitly authorizes game integration. No
			experience-principle change is proposed. Earlier eight-item capacity
			sketches are superseded by the five decided equipment/weapon slots; the
			rejected explorations stay available as requested.
		</p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				Promote the accepted hull, window, mounts and corrected stabilizers into
				one shared Fiddler definition, with schema validation and fitting rules.
			</li>
			<li>
				Connect the two engine banks and four turning thrusters to real thrust,
				energy, inertia, collision bounds and plume outputs. Preserve other
				ships.
			</li>
			<li>
				Add Fiddler to the Hangar and game selection, keep old saved builds
				readable, and expose Test flight with an editable trial loadout.
			</li>
			<li>
				Check geometry, compatibility, symmetry, push–turn separation, energy
				and save/flight handoff; inspect the ship in the Hangar and fly it in
				the browser.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			The accepted shape and mount layout now have a shared production
			definition. The crossed right-wing face that left a triangular gap is
			repaired. The Hangar, flight drawing and study use that corrected surface
			geometry. All four integration slices are delivered locally. No change to
			global gravity or friction was needed.
		</p>
		<p>
			Verification: 149 unit tests pass, including seven Fiddler integration
			tests; type checks, lint, contracts and the production build pass. Browser
			review covered the nine-slot Hangar, required rear-pair changes,
			support-only compatibility, the centre fitting, returning from flight,
			straight thrust, linked and unlinked turning, and main-menu 05 with the
			decided starting fit. The shared instrument shows equal turning
			acceleration with linking off and on, while forward acceleration differs.
		</p>
		<p>
			Automated browser and Firebase suites were not run in this iteration. The
			designer has accepted the form and authorized this trial; handling,
			endurance and final balance still await flying by the designer. This is
			local working code, not a published release.
		</p>
		<p>
			<a href="/builder?chassis=fiddler">Open Fiddler in the Hangar</a>
			· <a href="/">Play from the main menu, slot 05</a>
		</p>
	</section>
	<details>
		<summary>Instrument integration history</summary>
		<p>
			These notes were moved from the Motion instrument spec. They record the
			earlier provisional-chassis proposal and subsequent production
			integration; they add no instrument requirements.
		</p>
		<p>
			Originally proposed by Fiddler 02.11–02.12 on 2026-11-03, while its mass,
			eight-equipment sketch and stabilizer layout were still provisional. That
			stage could not model Fiddler with the existing chassis.
		</p>
		<p>
			The designer subsequently accepted the fixed Fiddler hull, 187 mass, four
			main engines, four built-in stabilizers and five equipment/weapon slots,
			and authorized game integration in 03–04. Fiddler is now a validated
			production loadout and can use the existing instrument document and real
			force calculation. The study embeds linked-off and linked-on Fiddler
			examples using the same starting fit. The ordinary instrument limits still
			apply: supplied power, no planetary gravity, no survival endurance claim.
			Actual energy and collision behavior is tested in flight.
		</p>
		<p>
			The broader proposal to author arbitrary provisional chassis, stabilizer
			geometry and independent force tuning inside instrument documents remains
			unimplemented. It is no longer a prerequisite for comparing this accepted
			Fiddler configuration.
		</p>
	</details>
</StudyLayout>

